[
    {
        "content": "<p><code>Sup { d ∈ ℝ≥0∞ | a ≥ b + d}</code>, which differs from <code>ENNReal</code>'s <code>a - b</code> only at a=b=∞.<br>\nIt has its own similar set of laws, e.g. an unqualified version of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ENNReal.sub_iSup#doc\">docs#ENNReal.sub_iSup</a> and a qualified version of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ENNReal.sub_iInf#doc\">docs#ENNReal.sub_iInf</a> . Afaict mathlib doesn't have it but maybe the code is out there...? <span aria-label=\"fingers crossed\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f91e\" role=\"img\" title=\"fingers crossed\">:fingers_crossed:</span> </p>\n<p>(Context: sup-inf-sup-... towers for Riesz / Hahn-Banach type constructions: separating hyperplanes and sandwich theorems and so forth. The analogue for division appears to be <code>(a⁻¹ * b)⁻¹</code>, which can make good use of existing <code>simp</code>s .)</p>",
        "id": 586254602,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776495575
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So, you want some version of subtraction such that <code>⊤ - ⊤ = ⊤</code>?</p>",
        "id": 586255214,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1776496216
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Technically yes</p>",
        "id": 586255379,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776496403
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you define this on <code>WithTop ℝ</code> instead of <code>ℝ≥0∞</code> then I think this is just <code>a + (-b)</code>. So you could perhaps compose that with some map <code>WithTop ℝ → ℝ≥0∞</code>?</p>",
        "id": 586255412,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1776496446
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Though for my use case what really matters is the way it's defined</p>",
        "id": 586255416,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776496449
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I do get the feeling this is a very specific thing to ask for. You sure you want this and can't just work with subtraction?</p>",
        "id": 586255441,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1776496484
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My ideal world would be coercing back to ordinary subtraction as quickly as possible</p>",
        "id": 586255658,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776496668
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The sort of constructions i'm interested in have shapes like <code>inf {a | ∀ b ∃ c ∀ d : Multinomial(a,b,c,d) ≥ 1}</code></p>",
        "id": 586255711,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776496711
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not sure I follow. Can you give an explicit example where this sort of subtraction would come up?</p>",
        "id": 586255784,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1776496782
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Let me try to find a MWE</p>",
        "id": 586255890,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776496901
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Given  <code>f : [0,1] → ℝ≥0∞</code>, call it <em>superlinear</em> (\"concave\") if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(𝔼[X]) ≥ 𝔼[f(X)]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">E</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">X</span><span class=\"mclose\">])</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">E</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">X</span><span class=\"mclose\">)]</span></span></span></span> and <em>sublinear</em> (\"convex\") for the order dual.</p>\n<p>There's a natural way of combining two superlinear functions:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊓</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">)(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">\\</span><span class=\"n\">mapsto</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">\\</span><span class=\"n\">inf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">\\</span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">\\</span><span class=\"n\">mid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">\\</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and two sublinear functions</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>↦</mo><mi>sup</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(f ⊔ g)(x) \\mapsto \\sup \\{f(p) + g(1-p) \\mid 0 ≤ p ≤ 1\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⊔</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">g</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">↦</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">sup</span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10764em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">g</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7804em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8304em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>(EDIT: typo in argument to <code>g</code>)</p>",
        "id": 586256600,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776497696
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(in my particular case, I care about Scott continuity so it's difficult to merely dualise past the inf/sup distinction)</p>",
        "id": 586256700,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776497821
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><del>....ok I give up on getting the tex to render</del></p>",
        "id": 586256741,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776497882
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The subtraction occurs because I'm trying to construct very specific super/sublinear functions such that any \"separating hyperplane\" (linear function between them) satisfies lies within some very small convex region</p>",
        "id": 586256933,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776498169
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>in particular I want to study functions that satisfy laws like <code>f(x,y) ≥ 1 ⟺ r*x ≤ s*x + t*x*y</code> or the various duals, and use those as building blocks for other functions</p>",
        "id": 586257322,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776498657
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Did you try changing the definition of subtraction in mathlib and seeing what happened?</p>",
        "id": 586257577,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1776499013
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(I misquoted the construction above and zulip is being mean about \"previous hash value\" :()</p>",
        "id": 586257578,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776499013
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Wouldn't that break all the existing lemmas?</p>",
        "id": 586257614,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776499041
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The existing inf construction is nicer to work with, it has 0 as a identity and sub-self-eq-zero</p>",
        "id": 586257702,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776499170
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well the exercise would be to fix them and to find out if your definition is better.</p>\n<p>I know that there are good reasons for some of the decisions made about infinity  eg 0*infty=0 because that's the correct assumption for measure theory. But I don't know if the community is wedded to its choice of infty-infty.</p>",
        "id": 586257729,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1776499198
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh this is not meant as a claim about better. I want to work with both at once</p>",
        "id": 586257764,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776499224
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh gosh! Then just define <code>sub'</code> I guess!</p>",
        "id": 586257792,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1776499258
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>that is Plan B! plan A is the fading tenuous hope that some other library I haven't heard of has already implemented it :P</p>",
        "id": 586257824,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776499295
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry, should have been clearer in the original post: I meant to frame my question as \"is there code for the thing I am describing?\"</p>",
        "id": 586258081,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1776499601
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"459227\">Violeta Hernández</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Sup.20.7B.20d.20.E2.88.88.20.E2.84.9D.E2.89.A50.E2.88.9E.20.7C.20a.20.E2.89.A5.20b.20.2B.20d.7D/near/586255214\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>So, you want some version of subtraction such that <code>⊤ - ⊤ = ⊤</code>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A version of subtraction that (as a monotone <code>R × R^od -&gt; R</code>) is sInf-preserving in its first argument and \"iSup\"-preserving  in the second,  <em>if</em> my napkin math holds up<br>\nI'm not sure if that's meaningful outside of linear orders like ENat and ENNReal and their direct sums</p>",
        "id": 586271191,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
